.\" lulibre(1). The guides in docs/ say more; :help inside lulibre reads them.
.TH LULIBRE 1 "October 2026" "lulibre 0.2.0-alpha.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lulibre \- look after the metadata of a calibre library, from the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lulibre
.RB [ \-L
.IR library ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR confdir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR name=value ]...
.RB [ \-\-clean ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ args ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lulibre
reads a calibre library (its
.I metadata.db
and the book files) and helps put its metadata right: it reads the first
pages of every file into a search index, proposes titles, authors, subjects,
dates, publishers and identifiers from what the files themselves say,
looks books up by identifier, finds duplicates and records without files,
and takes files from a sync folder into the library.
.PP
It never writes to the library itself. Every change goes to calibre through
.BR calibredb (1),
is recorded with the value it replaced, and can be undone.
.PP
With no
.I command
it opens the interactive interface: a list of the books, a preview of the
file under the cursor, and screens for editing and review. There,
.B ?
shows the keys under headings that open and close, with a search;
.BI :help " topic"
shows what the guides say about a word.
.SH COMMANDS
Each takes
.B \-h
for its own options.
.TP
.BR index " [" \-\-pages " \fIN\fR] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-retry "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Read the first pages of every file into the search index. Files that have
not changed are skipped, so it is cheap to run again and safe to interrupt.
.TP
.BR query " [" \-f " \fIcols\fR] [" \-n " \fIN\fR] [" \-c "] [" \-H "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
List the books a filter matches, one to a line, tab-separated.
.B \-c
prints only how many.
.TP
.BR show " [" \-p " \fIpage\fR] [" \-l "] \fIid\fR"
One book: its metadata, its files, the indexed text of a page.
.TP
.B stats
What is missing across the library, each line with the filter behind it.
.TP
.BR propose " [" \-\-only " \fIfields\fR] [" \-\-fresh "] [" \-\-over | \-\-gaps "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Propose metadata from the files themselves. Nothing is changed: the
proposals wait for review in the interface.
.TP
.BR enrich " [" \-\-plan "] [" \-\-offline "] [" \-\-titles "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Look books up by arXiv number, DOI or ISBN and propose what the records
say.
.B \-\-plan
says what would be asked of whom and asks nothing;
.B \-\-offline
proposes only from what was fetched before.
.TP
.BR datasets " [" list " | " fetch " \fIname\fR... | " add " \fIfile\fR...]"
Databases kept on this machine so that books can be looked up without
asking anybody: arXiv's records, Open Library's editions and authors,
zbMATH's links to arXiv. With no argument it says what there is and asks
which to fetch. It can be run again whenever.
.TP
.BR arxiv\-titles " [\fIfilter\fR]"
Find books' arXiv numbers by title in arXiv's snapshot kept on this
machine, asking nobody; a paper counts only when the book's pages show its
title and people.
.TP
.BR verify " [" \-\-list " title|authors|ids|unread] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Hold the library against its files: whether the opening pages show each
book's title and name its authors, and where its identifiers stand.
Changes nothing.
.TP
.BR recheck " [" \-\-list " | " \-\-propose " | " \-\-apply "] [" \-\-since " \fIsnap\fR] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Go back over what lulibre wrote, with each book's file deciding: titles and
authors a record replaced that the pages do not bear out go back, and
identifiers of cited or other works come off.
.B \-\-apply
takes a snapshot first. Typed values are left alone.
.TP
.BR snapshot " [\fIname\fR | " list " | " plan " \fIsnap\fR | " rollback " \fIsnap\fR | " retire " \fIsnap\fR | " import " \fIfile\fR]"
Points in the journal of changes that the library can be put back to.
.B rollback
puts back what lulibre changed since (with
.BR \-\-exact ,
what was changed outside it too), after taking a snapshot to roll forward
to; without a screen it needs
.BR \-\-yes .
.B plan
says what it would do.
.B import
makes a snapshot from an older copy of calibre's
.IR metadata.db .
.TP
.BR refs " [" \-\-force "] [\fIfilter\fR | " all ]
Read what the books cite, from the bibliography at the end of each file.
.TP
.BR sync " [" add " | " move " | " drop "] [\fIfilter\fR | " all ]
The sync folder: list the files waiting there, add them to the library,
add and remove them, or drop them.
.TP
.B dupes
Books whose files are the same, byte for byte.
.TP
.B orphans
Records with no file, files with no record, broken links.
.TP
.BR bib " [\fIfilter\fR]"
BibTeX for the books a filter matches.
.TP
.BR ocr " [\fIfilter\fR | " all ]
Read text from page images with
.BR tesseract (1).
.TP
.BR run " \fIscript\fR [\fIargs\fR]"
Run a Lua script without the interface: a path, or the name of a script in
the configuration directory or of a bundled one.
.TP
.BR config " [" \-\-write " | " \-\-path ]
Print the configuration as a Lua file, every option with what it does.
.B \-\-write
saves it as a first
.IR init.lua .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-L " dir"
The calibre library. Otherwise
.BR LULIBRE_LIBRARY ,
the option
.B library
of
.IR init.lua ,
or the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-o " name=value"
Set an option for this run.
.B "lulibre \-o help"
lists them all.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
The configuration directory.
.TP
.B \-\-clean
Do not load
.IR init.lua .
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and the number of the build.
.SH FILTERS
One language everywhere a filter is taken. Terms separated by spaces must
all hold;
.B or
starts an alternative; a leading
.B \-
negates.
.PP
.nf
    word  "a phrase"            in title, authors or tags
    author:erdos  subject:number-theory  year:>1990  fmt:djvu
    title:~^%d+   field:=exact   field:^prefix   field:none
    text:"class field"          the indexed pages
    no:author  no:date  has:doi  is:marked  is:filetitle
    sort:-added                 the order
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lulibre/init.lua
The configuration: plain Lua, loaded at startup.
.B "lulibre config \-\-write"
writes a first one. Scripts of your own go in
.I scripts/
beside it, modules in
.IR lua/ .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lulibre/
The index (one file for each library) and beside it its history
.RI ( <index>.history.db :
the journal of changes and the snapshots), the databases
.B "lulibre datasets"
fetches, and files dropped from the sync folder.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LULIBRE_LIBRARY
The calibre library, when
.B \-L
is not given.
.TP
.B LULIBRE_CONFIG_DIR
The configuration directory, when
.B \-C
is not given.
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the data and configuration folders are looked for.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command or a script fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR calibredb (1),
.BR calibre\-server (1)
.PP
The guides installed with lulibre (workflows, keys, filters, configuration,
api, development):
.BI ":help " topic
in the interface, or the files of
.I docs/
under its data directory.
